export const DATA_95: ReadingTest[] = [
  { id: "rd-171", num: 171, title: "Đề 171", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Heavy Rainfall and the Growing Risk of Landslides", paragraphs: ["In many hilly and mountainous regions around the world, unusually heavy rainfall has become a growing concern for local communities. When rain falls in large amounts over a short period, water can quickly soak into hillsides that are already close to their saturation point. Once the ground can no longer absorb any more water, the extra weight and reduced friction inside the soil sometimes trigger a sudden downward movement of earth and rock known as a landslide. Such events can happen with very little warning, and in some cases entire hillsides give way within minutes.", "Several factors combine to make some slopes more vulnerable than others. Steep terrain naturally increases the risk, since gravity pulls loosened soil downhill more easily on a sharp incline than on a gentle one. Prolonged or repeated rainfall over several days, rather than a single short burst, is often more dangerous because it gradually saturates deeper layers of soil. Human activity plays a role as well: when forests are cleared for farming or construction, the tree roots that once held soil together disappear, leaving the slope far less stable. Poorly planned roads and construction projects can also cut into hillsides in ways that remove natural support, quietly increasing the danger long before any rain falls.", "The consequences of a major landslide can be severe. Homes built on or near affected slopes are sometimes buried or swept away entirely, and roads connecting villages to larger towns may be blocked for days or weeks, cutting off access to food, medical care, and other essential supplies. Farmland can be destroyed in a matter of seconds, wiping out a family's income for an entire season or longer. In the most serious cases, landslides claim human lives, particularly when they strike at night while residents are asleep and unable to react quickly. Even communities that escape direct damage often face rising insurance costs and falling property values afterward, since the risk of a future slide changes how outsiders view the area.", "In response, many governments and communities have invested in ways to reduce the danger. Early warning systems that measure rainfall and soil movement can alert residents before a slope begins to fail, giving people precious time to evacuate. Planting trees and other vegetation on bare slopes helps restore some of the natural stability that deforestation had removed. Engineers also build drainage channels to carry excess water away from vulnerable hillsides rather than letting it pool and soak in. Local authorities frequently distribute simple safety guidelines, encouraging residents to watch for warning signs such as new cracks in the ground or tilting trees near their homes.", "Despite this progress, significant gaps remain, especially in rural or lower-income areas. Installing and maintaining sensor-based warning systems is costly, and many isolated villages still rely on word-of-mouth rather than any formal alert system. Some scientists warn that shifting weather patterns may bring heavier and more frequent rainstorms to regions unaccustomed to them, meaning slopes considered stable for generations could face new risks in the years ahead. Closing this gap, most experts agree, will require steady long-term investment rather than a one-time response to a single disaster."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Rural mountainous territories demonstrate superior resilience against unexpected landslide hazards", "Sensor-based detection technologies have effectively eliminated landslide vulnerabilities across high-risk zones", "Heavy rainfall can trigger landslides, and several combined causes and solutions are described", "Catastrophic ground slope collapses exclusively impact untouched ecosystems free from human construction"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, why does prolonged rainfall over several days tend to be more dangerous than a single short, heavy rainstorm?", options: ["It weakens tree roots directly rather than affecting the soil", "It increases the steepness of hillsides over a short time", "It gradually saturates deeper layers of soil beneath the surface", "It causes roads to flood before any soil movement begins"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what can happen to communities that escape direct landslide damage?", options: ["They usually see property values increase due to safety upgrades", "They may face rising insurance costs and falling property values afterward", "They are required to relocate within a fixed period of time", "They typically receive immediate government compensation for potential future risk"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a way to reduce landslide danger?", options: ["Planting trees and vegetation to restore slope stability", "Distributing safety guidelines about warning signs to residents", "Building drainage channels to carry excess water away", "Relocating entire villages to newly built government towns"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"trigger\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["trace", "cause", "erode", "worsen"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"restore\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["remove", "replace", "renew", "reduce"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The phrase \"Such events\" in paragraph 1 refers to _______.", options: ["hillsides", "heavy rainstorms", "landslides", "warning signs"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what do some scientists warn about shifting weather patterns?", options: ["They primarily intensify precipitation hazards within zones traditionally accustomed to heavy monsoon rains", "They have rendered technological monitoring networks obsolete across remote mountainous communities", "They may bring heavier and more frequent storms to unaccustomed regions", "They will produce a sustained worldwide reduction in total annual rainfall levels across all continents"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage about villages that rely on word-of-mouth rather than a formal alert system?", options: ["They experience minimal slope failure risks due to their geographical isolation from urban centers", "They achieve equivalent emergency reaction speeds compared to communities equipped with automated sensors", "They likely receive warnings later than villages with sensor-based systems", "They deliberately favor informal communication models because traditional methods outperform technology"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What is the author's overall purpose in writing this passage?", options: ["To criticize governments for entirely ignoring rural landslide warning needs", "To explain how landslides occur and describe efforts to reduce danger", "To argue that landslides can never be prevented by any method", "To promote only one company's landslide warning technology as the best"], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "Comparing Experiences of Anti-Asian Discrimination in the United States and Canada", paragraphs: ["Both the United States and Canada are home to large and diverse Asian communities, and both countries have, at various points over the past two decades, seen public conversations intensify around discrimination directed at people of Asian descent. Comparative researchers studying these conversations have generally cautioned against assuming that discrimination looks the same, or occurs at the same rate, simply because the two countries share a long border and, in some respects, comparable immigration histories. Beneath surface similarities, several studies suggest, lie meaningful differences in how discrimination is experienced, reported, and addressed.", "Understanding present-day patterns arguably requires looking backward first, since both nations built exclusionary immigration policy directly into law during roughly the same historical period. The United States barred most Chinese immigration outright beginning in 1882, a policy that remained in force for over sixty years, while Canada imposed a steep head tax on Chinese immigrants before adopting its own near-total exclusion law in 1923. These policies were not merely historical footnotes, several historians argue; the legal architecture built to justify them shaped public attitudes in ways that echoed for generations afterward, even once the laws themselves were formally repealed.", "More recent survey data complicates any simple before-and-after narrative. Large-scale surveys conducted since 2020 have found that a substantial majority of Asian American respondents report having personally experienced some form of discrimination, ranging from subtle social slights to overt verbal harassment, with a smaller but still significant share reporting physical intimidation or assault. Canadian surveys covering the same period show comparable proportions reporting verbal harassment, yet somewhat lower proportions reporting physical incidents — a gap researchers have not fully explained, though some point to differences in urban population density and policing practices between the two countries.", "One frequently proposed explanation for divergent public responses lies in each country's official approach to cultural diversity. Canada adopted multiculturalism as formal government policy in 1971, embedding the concept into federal law and public education in ways that the United States, which has never adopted an equivalent national policy, largely has not. Whether this difference meaningfully reduces discrimination itself remains disputed; some researchers argue that official multiculturalism mainly shapes how incidents are discussed and reported publicly rather than how frequently they occur in the first place.", "The economic dimension of discrimination has received somewhat less public attention than incidents of harassment, though the data here is arguably just as revealing. In both countries, Asian professionals are frequently associated with the so-called model minority stereotype, an assumption of uniform academic and economic success that, researchers note, obscures considerable internal variation and can itself function as a barrier — subtly justifying the underrepresentation of Asian employees in senior leadership roles even at firms and universities where entry-level representation is comparatively high. Wage and promotion data from both countries show measurable gaps between Asian employees and their non-Asian peers at senior levels, gaps that narrow only modestly once education and years of experience are statistically controlled for.", "Community organizations in both countries have responded by building reporting networks intended to capture incidents that official crime statistics tend to undercount , since many victims, researchers find, do not report incidents to police at all. These grassroots reporting systems , while methodologically different from official statistics, have in both countries produced the most detailed available data on lower-level harassment — the kind of incident too minor, in the view of many victims, to justify a formal police report but frequent enough, taken together, to shape everyday experience.", "Taken as a whole, the comparative research suggests that formal policy differences between the two countries have shaped the public conversation around anti-Asian discrimination more clearly than they have shaped its underlying prevalence. Whether Canada's longer-standing multiculturalism policy will, over a longer time horizon, produce measurably different outcomes than the American approach remains, most researchers agree, an open question that current data cannot yet answer with confidence."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Discrimination against Asian people occurs only within workplace settings rather than in public life generally", "Discrimination against Asian people differs across the US and Canada mainly in how it is reported", "The United States has never enacted any exclusionary immigration law aimed at Asian immigrants historically", "Canada's official policy of multiculturalism has entirely eliminated discrimination against Asian communities in the country"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, in what year did the United States begin barring most Chinese immigration?", options: ["1971", "1923", "2020", "1882"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what notable difference did researchers find between Asian American and Canadian survey respondents?", options: ["Both groups reported completely identical proportions of physical and verbal incidents overall", "Canadian respondents reported somewhat lower proportions of physical incidents than Asian American respondents", "Canadian respondents reported far higher proportions of physical incidents than Asian American respondents", "Asian American respondents reported no verbal harassment at all in recent surveys"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as an effect associated with the model minority stereotype?", options: ["It can function as a barrier to senior leadership representation", "It obscures considerable variation among Asian individuals and communities", "It is associated with measurable wage gaps at senior levels", "It guarantees automatic promotion into senior leadership positions quickly"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"obscures\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["hides", "promotes", "observes", "erases"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"undercount\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["overreport", "underreport", "discount", "eliminate"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The phrase \"These grassroots reporting systems\" in paragraph 6 refers to _______.", options: ["community reporting networks", "government multiculturalism programs", "official crime statistics", "the police"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What remains uncertain about the effect of Canada's multiculturalism policy?", options: ["Whether the legislative framework applies uniformly across all sovereign provinces nationwide", "Whether Canadian authorities instituted this national strategy prior to similar American initiatives", "Whether the cultural guidelines have been formally integrated into public education and federal law", "Whether it produces measurably different discrimination outcomes than the American approach over time"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage about the relationship between official multiculturalism policy and the actual frequency of discrimination?", options: ["A formal policy always reduces discrimination frequency more effectively than informal social norms do", "A formal policy has no relationship whatsoever to how discrimination is publicly discussed", "A formal policy mainly affects immigration law rather than public discussion or discrimination frequency", "A formal policy may shape public discussion without necessarily reducing how often discrimination occurs"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What is the author's overall purpose in writing this passage?", options: ["To compare research findings on anti-Asian discrimination in the US and Canada carefully", "To prove that discrimination against Asian communities no longer exists in either country", "To argue that Canada's approach to discrimination is unquestionably superior to the American one", "To criticize individual researchers publicly for disagreeing about the causes of discrimination"], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "Shifting Patterns in Lung Cancer Diagnosis and Treatment", paragraphs: ["Lung cancer continues to account for more cancer deaths worldwide than any other single malignancy, a statistic that has remained essentially unchanged for decades even as survival rates for many other cancers have improved. Much of this burden traces back to how the disease is typically discovered: because early-stage tumors rarely produce symptoms, most cases are diagnosed only after the cancer has spread, at which point treatment options narrow considerably. Smoking remains the largest risk factor by a wide margin, yet the diagnosed population has grown noticeably more heterogeneous in recent years, with a growing share of cases occurring in people who have never smoked.", "Screening with low-dose computed tomography has been shown, in large trials, to reduce mortality among people meeting specific age and smoking-history criteria, yet uptake remains far lower than officials had hoped. Part of the hesitation stems from an unresolved tension: while catching tumors earlier improves outcomes for many patients, a nontrivial proportion of screen-detected abnormalities would, evidence suggests, never have caused clinical harm if left undetected, exposing those patients to biopsies and unnecessary surgery without any survival benefit. Distinguishing which nodules genuinely warrant aggressive follow-up from which can safely be monitored remains, several oncologists note, a persistently unresolved question.", "Diagnosis today increasingly involves more than confirming a tumor's presence. Molecular testing for mutations in genes such as EGFR, ALK, and KRAS has, over roughly fifteen years, transformed treatment from a largely uniform approach into something considerably more individualized, since tumors carrying particular mutations often respond dramatically to drugs that offer little benefit to tumors lacking them. This shift has proven especially consequential for the never-smoker patients mentioned earlier, whose tumors are disproportionately likely to carry precisely the mutations these targeted therapies address — reshaping how clinicians approach a population that earlier screening guidelines, built around smoking history, were not designed to capture.", "Immunotherapy works by a fundamentally different mechanism, prompting a patient's immune system to attack cancer cells rather than targeting a specific mutation. Checkpoint inhibitors have measurably extended survival for a meaningful subset of patients, sometimes producing responses persisting for years in cases once considered untreatable. Response, however, remains markedly heterogeneous : biomarkers such as PD-L1 expression offer some predictive value, but a substantial minority of low-expressing tumors nonetheless respond well, while some high-expressing tumors fail to respond at all, leaving clinicians without a fully reliable way to predict who benefits most.", "None of these advances, it should be emphasized, has been distributed evenly across the population that might benefit. Access to screening, molecular testing, and the newest agents correlates strongly with insurance coverage, proximity to specialized centers, and, in several studies, race — disparities that compound rather than merely coexist, since a patient without molecular testing cannot, by definition, benefit from a targeted therapy regardless of whether their tumor would have responded well. Rural populations face a documented combination of lower screening rates and longer distances to capable centers, a gap several researchers argue has only widened as treatment has grown more specialized.", "Prevention efforts present a genuinely mixed global picture. Sustained tobacco taxation and indoor smoking bans have coincided with declining incidence in several high-income countries, though researchers caution that correlation alone cannot establish how much these specific policies caused versus other concurrent shifts. In other regions, incidence continues to rise alongside growing tobacco use, complicating any simple narrative of improvement. Attention has also turned toward non-smoking risk factors — residential radon and outdoor air pollution chief among them — which researchers believe account for a disproportionate share of never-smoker cases, though quantifying their precise contribution remains considerably harder than measuring a well-documented exposure like cigarette smoke.", "Looking ahead, emerging technologies have generated considerable optimism, though most researchers temper that optimism with caution. Liquid biopsy techniques, detecting tumor DNA fragments in blood, may eventually allow earlier detection without invasive sampling, while AI tools applied to imaging show promise in flagging abnormalities a radiologist might miss. Neither technology, however, has yet been validated at the scale required for routine use, and several specialists have cautioned against a pattern of overpromising in early coverage, noting that the gap between a promising finding and a reliable, equitably accessible tool has historically proven wider than initial enthusiasm suggests."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Lung cancer care has advanced scientifically, yet access to these advances remains uneven", "Immunotherapy has fully replaced molecular testing as the standard for every lung cancer case", "Cigarette smoking is the only meaningful risk factor across every group of patients", "Every recent lung cancer treatment advance has reached all patient populations equally worldwide"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what tension complicates the case for widespread lung cancer screening?", options: ["Earlier detection helps patients, but some abnormalities detected would never have caused real harm", "Screening programs have been shown in every trial to increase mortality among high-risk patients", "Public health officials have found screening uptake exceeds expectations in most affected countries", "Screening criteria based on age and smoking history have already been abandoned by all programs"], answer: 0 }, { q: "Why has molecular testing become especially relevant to the growing population of never-smoker patients mentioned earlier in the passage?", options: ["Never-smoker tumors usually lack genetic mutations detectable by modern tests", "Never-smoker tumors respond far better to immunotherapy than molecular testing", "Never-smoker patients are excluded entirely from standard screening protocols", "Never-smoker tumors often have mutations targeted by specific drugs"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as part of lung cancer prevention efforts?", options: ["Mandatory annual chest X-rays nationwide", "Comprehensive bans on indoor smoking", "Sustained taxation on tobacco products", "Residential radon gas exposure levels"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"heterogeneous\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["varied", "inconsistent", "hereditary", "targeted"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"overpromising\" in paragraph 7 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["exaggerating", "validating", "overlooking", "guaranteeing"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The phrase \"This shift\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["the move toward individualized, mutation-based treatment", "the decline in overall smoking rates", "the age-based screening guidelines used earlier", "the steadily growing population of never-smokers"], answer: 0 }, { q: "Why does the passage suggest that molecular testing access disparities may compound rather than simply coexist with other disparities?", options: ["A patient lacking molecular testing access cannot benefit from a well-suited therapy", "Targeted therapies are equally effective regardless of whether molecular testing was ever performed", "Molecular testing is now universally available regardless of insurance coverage or location", "Rural patients receive molecular testing more consistently than urban specialized center patients"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What can be inferred from paragraph 7 about the likely near-term impact of liquid biopsy and AI-assisted imaging on equitable lung cancer care?", options: ["They are expected to replace tissue-based molecular testing within a few years", "Their actual benefit for all patients remains uncertain despite current research", "They have already eliminated existing economic disparities in access to care", "They are considered fully validated and ready for immediate clinical use"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What is the author's overall attitude toward recent advances in lung cancer care?", options: ["Dismissive, treating recent scientific advances as largely irrelevant to patient outcomes", "Fully confident that modern innovations have successfully solved all remaining issues", "Uniformly pessimistic about the long-term clinical value of current screening methods", "Cautiously optimistic, noting progress alongside open questions"], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "What the Big Bang Theory Does and Does Not Claim", paragraphs: ["That the universe originated in a single, definable event — an image evoked by the phrase 'the Big Bang' — is, strictly speaking, a more contested characterization than popular accounts suggest. What cosmologists mean by the term is narrower and more hedged than the everyday image implies: a description, grounded in extrapolating well-tested physical laws backward in time, of how a hot, dense, rapidly expanding early universe evolved into the cosmos observed today, rather than a claim, fully substantiated by current physics, about what — if anything — preceded or caused that initial state.", "Confidence in this picture rests on several independent lines of evidence rather than any single observation. Foremost, historically, was the discovery that distant galaxies recede from our own at speeds roughly proportional to their distance — a relationship first documented in the late 1920s and now understood to reflect space itself expanding, not galaxies moving through static space. Run the expansion backward mathematically, and one arrives, under reasonable assumptions, at an epoch when all observable matter would have been compressed into an extraordinarily hot, dense state.", "It was not this recession data alone, however, but a largely accidental discovery decades later that most decisively corroborated the emerging picture. In 1965, researchers testing a radio antenna detected a faint, uniform background of microwave radiation arriving from every direction in the sky — radiation interpreted as the cooled remnant of light released roughly 380,000 years after the universe's earliest moments, once it had cooled enough for atoms to form and light to travel freely. Minor variations in this background's temperature are understood to trace the density fluctuations from which galaxies eventually formed, linking the earliest observable universe to the large-scale structure seen billions of years later.", "A further, independently derived line of support comes from predictions about the relative abundance of the universe's lightest elements. Calculations describing nuclear reactions in the first few minutes after the hot early state predict specific ratios of hydrogen, helium, and trace lithium that should have formed before stars existed to synthesize heavier elements. Observed hydrogen and helium abundances match these predictions closely enough to count, in the view of most physicists, as strong evidence; the predicted lithium abundance, however, diverges from what telescopes observe by a margin researchers have neither dismissed as error nor fully explained — a discrepancy known, with evident understatement, as the 'lithium problem.'", "Even granting this evidence, the standard picture left certain features of the observed universe — its large-scale uniformity and geometric flatness — without a clear explanation, prompting the proposal, in the early 1980s, of a still-earlier phase of extraordinarily rapid expansion known as cosmic inflation. Inflationary models account elegantly for these puzzling features and have gained support from measurements of the microwave background's fine structure; whether inflation occurred in the manner any single model proposes, however, remains genuinely unsettled, since multiple variants fit current data adequately and no observation yet favors one definitively.", "Perhaps the most misunderstood aspect of the theory concerns what it claims about the very beginning. Extrapolating known laws backward far enough produces a mathematical singularity — a point of infinite density at which those equations cease to yield meaningful predictions. Whether this artifact corresponds to an actual physical event, a literal beginning of time, or merely marks a boundary beyond which physics stops applying and some unknown theory would describe what came before, is a question current cosmology cannot answer with confidence; physicists are, on this point, considerably more circumspect than the popular narrative of a single originating explosion suggests.", "This acknowledged limitation has not stopped more speculative proposals from circulating, some better supported than others. A minority of theorists, extending inflationary models to their logical conclusion, have proposed that inflation might never fully end everywhere at once, generating not one universe but a continually branching multitude of separate regions — a scenario described as eternal inflation and popularly associated with a broader multiverse. Such proposals remain, by the admission of many who take them seriously, largely unfalsifiable given current observational capacity, and mainstream cosmology treats them as intriguing possibilities warranting further development rather than established components of the standard model.", "Considered as a whole, the evidence supporting an early hot, dense, expanding universe is genuinely robust for describing cosmic evolution from a small fraction of a second after that state onward — robust enough that the framework is treated as settled science. What remains considerably less settled, and what popular treatments frequently elide, is any confident claim about what the singularity itself represents physically, or what — if a meaningful 'before' even applies — might have preceded it; on these deepest questions, most working cosmologists hold a position of disciplined uncertainty rather than confident narrative."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Multiple independent evidence lines support an early expanding universe, but its ultimate origin remains open", "Cosmologists have definitively proven that a single explosive event created the universe out of nothing", "Current physics has convincingly ruled out any possibility that the universe had a definite beginning", "The cosmic microwave background alone fully settles every remaining question about the universe's origin"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What role did the cosmic microwave background play relative to the earlier redshift observations?", options: ["It directly contradicted conclusions cosmologists had drawn from earlier redshift observations", "It first suggested cosmic expansion decades before redshift observations were ever made", "It provided decisive corroboration for a picture the redshift data had already suggested", "It was discovered specifically to test predictions made by redshift measurements"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does the predicted abundance of lithium compare to that of hydrogen and helium in relation to observation?", options: ["Lithium predictions match observation perfectly while hydrogen and helium predictions remain disputed", "All three predicted element abundances match observed values equally closely and without exception", "None of the three predicted element abundances have ever matched any observed values", "Hydrogen and helium predictions match observation closely, but the lithium prediction remains unexplained"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned in connection with the theory of cosmic inflation?", options: ["Receiving support from background fine structure", "Helping explain the universe's large-scale uniformity", "Helping explain the universe's geometric flatness", "Being confirmed by direct gravitational detection"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"circumspect\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["cautious", "certain", "circular", "singular"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"unfalsifiable\" in paragraph 7 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["unjustifiable", "unobservable", "untestable", "untrue"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The phrase \"This acknowledged limitation\" in paragraph 7 refers to _______.", options: ["the accidental discovery of background radiation described earlier", "cosmology's inability to explain what preceded the singularity", "the earlier discrepancy in predicted lithium abundance levels", "the multiple competing inflationary models discussed earlier"], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which best characterizes what current cosmology can confidently claim about the universe's ultimate origin?", options: ["It can confirm that the singularity represents a literal physical beginning", "It can confirm with full certainty that nothing existed before the singularity", "It can describe the early dense state, but cannot confirm what preceded it", "It can rule out entirely any earlier physical process preceding the singularity"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred from paragraph 5 about the current scientific status of cosmic inflation?", options: ["It was proposed but has since contradicted all microwave background measurements", "It is widely supported as a concept, but its precise mechanism remains unsettled", "It has been fully confirmed in every detail by unanimous scientific agreement", "It has been entirely rejected due to a lack of supporting evidence"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What is the author's overall attitude toward popular narratives describing the Big Bang as a single, fully explained originating explosion?", options: ["Skeptical of oversimplified certainty, favoring a carefully qualified account of current physics", "Dismissive of all scientific evidence supporting an early expanding universe", "Fully supportive, treating popular narratives as accurate and complete summaries of physics", "Indifferent, expressing no view on how the theory is presented"], answer: 0 }] },
  ] },
  { id: "rd-172", num: 172, title: "Đề 172", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Understanding Ultraviolet Radiation and Sun Safety", paragraphs: ["Ultraviolet, or UV, radiation is a type of energy that comes from the sun. It cannot be seen by the human eye, but its effects on the body are easy to notice. Scientists usually divide UV radiation into a few different types, though most of what reaches the ground and affects our skin belongs to just two of these categories. When skin is exposed to UV rays for too long, it can quickly turn red and sore, a reaction most people simply call sunburn. Even on a cloudy day, a surprising amount of UV radiation can still reach the ground and affect unprotected skin.", "Repeated exposure to UV radiation over many years can damage the skin in ways that are not always immediately visible. Doctors have found clear links between long-term sun exposure and premature skin aging, including wrinkles and dark spots that appear earlier than they otherwise would. More seriously, years of unprotected exposure can raise the risk of skin cancer, since UV rays are able to harm the cells that make up the outer layer of skin. People with fair skin, light-colored eyes, or a family history of skin cancer tend to face a somewhat higher risk from the same amount of sun exposure. This is why health experts consistently encourage people to take sun protection seriously, even when the weather does not feel especially hot.", "At the same time, UV radiation is not entirely harmful. A small amount of regular sun exposure helps the human body produce vitamin D, a nutrient that supports strong bones and a healthy immune system. Without enough vitamin D, children in particular can develop weaker bones as they grow, which is one reason doctors rarely recommend avoiding the sun completely. Some doctors even use carefully controlled UV light to treat certain skin conditions in a hospital setting. Finding the right balance, then, is important: too little sun exposure can lead to low vitamin D levels, while too much can cause lasting skin damage.", "Fortunately, there are several simple ways to reduce the risks of UV exposure while still enjoying time outdoors. Wearing sunscreen with a strong sun protection factor, choosing long sleeves and a wide-brimmed hat, and staying in the shade during the middle of the day are all effective habits. Sunglasses that block UV rays can also protect the eyes, which are sensitive to sun damage as well. Reapplying sunscreen every couple of hours, especially after swimming or sweating, makes this protection considerably more reliable . Parents are often advised to be especially careful with young children, whose skin tends to burn more easily than adult skin does.", "To help people plan their outdoor activities safely, many weather services now report a daily UV index. This number tells people how strong the sun's UV radiation is expected to be on a given day, with higher numbers meaning greater risk. The index is often grouped into simple categories, such as low, moderate, high, and very high, so that people can quickly understand the day's risk level without needing to know the exact number. The UV index tends to rise around midday, and it can also be higher in summer, at high altitudes, or near reflective surfaces such as snow and water. Checking the UV index before going outside, much like checking the weather forecast, has become an easy habit that helps families make smarter choices about sun protection."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["The UV index is the only reliable method available for measuring skin damage", "UV radiation has both risks and benefits, and simple habits can help manage sun exposure safely", "UV radiation is only dangerous to people who spend time outdoors during winter months", "Doctors now recommend that everyone avoid all sun exposure completely, in every season"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what have doctors found regarding long-term unprotected sun exposure?", options: ["It has been shown to actually lower a person's overall future illness risk", "It is clearly linked to premature skin aging and a higher cancer risk", "It only becomes harmful after many consecutive years without any sunscreen at all", "It mainly affects the eyes rather than the skin over a long time"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why is a small amount of regular sun exposure considered helpful?", options: ["It is the only known way for the body to produce vitamin D", "It helps the body produce vitamin D, which supports bones and immunity", "It allows doctors to stop recommending sunscreen for healthy young adults", "It permanently removes any risk of developing skin cancer later in life"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a way to reduce UV exposure risks?", options: ["Staying in the shade during midday hours", "Wearing sunscreen with a strong protection factor", "Taking daily vitamin D supplements instead", "Choosing long sleeves and a wide-brimmed hat"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"damage\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["delay", "protect", "harm", "damp"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"reliable\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["dependable", "expensive", "relaxing", "reversible"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"it\" in paragraph 1 refers to _______.", options: ["a cloudy day", "unprotected skin", "ultraviolet radiation", "the human eye"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, when does the UV index tend to be higher?", options: ["Around midday, in summer, at high altitudes, or near reflective surfaces", "Only during the early morning hours before most people wake up", "Only in regions located very far from the Earth's equator", "Exclusively during winter months in areas that receive heavy snowfall"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage about checking the UV index before going outside?", options: ["It can help families make more informed choices about sun protection", "It has replaced the need for wearing sunscreen entirely", "It is only useful for people who already have skin conditions", "It guarantees complete protection from all UV-related skin damage"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What is the author's overall purpose in writing this passage?", options: ["To criticize weather services for reporting an inaccurate daily UV index", "To argue that sunscreen is unnecessary for most healthy people", "To promote a single specific brand of sunscreen over all others", "To explain UV radiation's effects and offer practical sun protection advice"], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "Sugary Beverages and the Rise of Childhood Obesity", paragraphs: ["Rates of childhood obesity have climbed steadily in many countries over the past few decades, and public health researchers increasingly point to one dietary factor as a particularly influential contributor: the regular consumption of sugar-sweetened beverages. Sodas, sweetened juices, and flavored milk drinks now make up a substantial share of many children's daily caloric intake, a shift that has occurred alongside, and some researchers argue partly because of, aggressive marketing aimed specifically at young audiences.", "What makes sugary drinks especially concerning, from a nutritional standpoint, is not simply their calorie content but how the body processes those calories. Unlike calories consumed through solid food, calories from sweetened beverages tend not to trigger the same feeling of fullness, meaning children who drink a sugary beverage with a meal often go on to consume roughly the same amount of solid food they would have eaten anyway. The additional liquid calories, in other words, are frequently consumed on top of normal food intake rather than replacing it, a pattern that several long-term studies have linked to steady, cumulative weight gain over time.", "Marketing practices have drawn particular scrutiny in recent years. Beverage companies have long directed a disproportionate share of advertising toward children and adolescents, using bright packaging, popular characters, and messaging strategically placed on television programs and websites that young audiences frequent. Industry representatives have generally maintained that such marketing merely builds brand awareness rather than directly driving consumption; public health advocates, by contrast, argue that repeated exposure to this kind of advertising measurably shapes children's beverage preferences well before they are old enough to critically evaluate the claims being made.", "In response to mounting evidence, a number of governments have introduced policies specifically targeting sugar-sweetened beverages. Several countries and cities have implemented taxes on sugary drinks, calculated either by volume or by sugar content, with the explicit goal of discouraging consumption through higher prices. Early evaluations of these taxes have generally found modest reductions in purchasing, though researchers remain divided over how much of that reduction reflects a genuine decline in consumption versus consumers simply switching to untaxed alternatives that may carry similarly high sugar content.", "Schools represent another frequent site of policy intervention, since children often obtain a meaningful share of their daily beverage intake through school cafeterias and vending machines. Many school districts have removed sugary drinks from vending machines entirely or replaced them with water, plain milk, and unsweetened alternatives. Compliance with these policies, however, varies considerably from district to district, and some researchers caution that removing sugary drinks from school settings alone accomplishes relatively little if children simply increase their consumption at home to compensate.", "Socioeconomic disparities further complicate the broader picture. Children from lower-income households are, according to several studies, disproportionately likely to consume sugar-sweetened beverages regularly, a pattern researchers attribute to a combination of factors: sugary drinks are often cheaper per calorie than healthier alternatives, are more heavily marketed in lower-income neighborhoods, and are more readily available in areas with limited access to fresh food. These same communities, researchers note, frequently have less access to the parks, recreational facilities, and safe outdoor spaces that might otherwise help offset excess caloric intake through physical activity.", "Taken together, the accumulated research suggests that addressing childhood obesity through beverage policy alone is unlikely to prove sufficient. Reducing sugary drink consumption, most public health experts now agree, works best as one component of a broader strategy that also addresses food marketing, physical activity access, and the underlying economic pressures that shape what families are realistically able to afford."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Sugary drink taxes have already eliminated childhood obesity in every country that adopted them", "School vending machine policies alone have fully resolved the childhood obesity problem", "Marketing has no measurable effect whatsoever on children's beverage preferences or consumption", "Sugary beverages contribute meaningfully to childhood obesity, but policy alone cannot fully solve it"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, why are liquid calories from sugary drinks considered especially concerning?", options: ["They often fail to trigger fullness, so children eat about the same solid food anyway", "They are absorbed into the bloodstream much more slowly than calories from solid foods", "They are typically consumed as a complete substitute for a balanced daily solid meal", "They contain a distinct form of sugar that is chemically absent from solid food sources"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, how do industry representatives and public health advocates differ in their view of beverage marketing aimed at children?", options: ["Both groups agree marketing directly and substantially increases beverage consumption", "Industry admits it drives consumption directly; advocates argue it has no real effect", "Industry says it builds brand awareness; advocates say it shapes preferences early on", "Both groups agree marketing has no measurable influence on children's preferences"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a reason lower-income households consume more sugary drinks?", options: ["Fresh food access tends to be more limited in these areas", "Government subsidies specifically fund sugary drink purchases there", "Sugary drinks are often cheaper per calorie than healthier options", "Sugary drinks are more heavily marketed in these neighborhoods"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"scrutiny\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["secrecy", "scarcity", "approval", "examination"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"compliance\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["adherence", "resistance", "complexity", "complaint"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The phrase \"such marketing\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["long-term scientific studies linking sugary drinks to steady cumulative weight gain over time", "advertising using bright packaging and popular characters aimed at children", "government tax systems calculated strictly by beverage volume or added sugar content", "school policies focused on replacing sugary drinks with plain milk and unsweetened options"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What concern do researchers raise about relying on taxes and school policies alone?", options: ["These policies have already proven entirely successful across all implementation sites nationwide", "Taxation consistently leads to an unexpected surge in overall sugary drink purchases", "Consumers or children may simply shift consumption to untaxed drinks or home settings", "School boards have uniformly rejected the enforcement of vending machine restrictions"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred from paragraph 6 about why beverage policy alone may not resolve disparities in childhood obesity?", options: ["Sugary drink consumption represents the sole dietary difference between varied income groups", "Lower-income families maintain a strong preference for sugary drinks regardless of pricing shifts", "Underlying economic and environmental factors extend well beyond beverage choice alone", "Recreational facilities and safe outdoor spaces are already distributed evenly across communities"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What is the author's overall purpose in writing this passage?", options: ["To prove that sugary drink taxation is the single most effective health policy available", "To examine how sugary drinks contribute to childhood obesity and evaluate policy responses", "To criticize parents directly for allowing children to consume sugar-sweetened beverages", "To argue that beverage corporations bear zero accountability for current health trends"], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "Detecting Distant Worlds: Exoplanets and the Question of Habitability", paragraphs: ["Confirmed discoveries of planets orbiting stars beyond our own sun have accelerated dramatically since the first such detection in the early 1990s, with astronomers now cataloguing several thousand confirmed exoplanets and thousands more candidates awaiting verification. This surge has renewed a much older question in a considerably more concrete form: rather than speculating abstractly about life elsewhere, researchers can now examine specific, real planets and ask whether conditions there might plausibly support it.", "Two detection methods account for the overwhelming majority of confirmed exoplanets, and each carries its own built-in bias. The transit method identifies planets by measuring the tiny, periodic dip in a star's brightness as an orbiting planet passes in front of it; this technique favors planets with orbits aligned so precisely with our line of sight that a transit is even visible, and it works best for planets that orbit close to their star, since closer orbits produce more frequent, easily confirmed transits. The radial velocity method instead detects the slight gravitational wobble a planet induces in its star, a technique that favors especially massive planets, since smaller worlds produce a wobble too subtle to reliably measure with current instruments.", "Central to the search for habitability is the concept of the habitable zone, the range of distances from a star within which a rocky planet could plausibly maintain liquid water on its surface. This concept, however, is considerably less straightforward than early popular accounts suggested. A planet's actual habitability depends on its atmosphere, its geological activity, and the specific type of star it orbits — planets orbiting small, dim red dwarf stars, for instance, must orbit so closely to remain within the habitable zone that they frequently become tidally locked, permanently presenting one hemisphere to their star, a configuration whose implications for habitability remain genuinely debated among specialists.", "Confirming that a planet actually harbors life, as opposed to merely occupying a promising orbit, requires detecting so-called biosignatures — atmospheric gases whose presence would be difficult to explain without biological activity. Oxygen and methane occurring together are frequently cited as a promising combination, since the two gases react with each other and would not be expected to coexist in significant quantities without some ongoing process replenishing them. Yet this apparent signature is not unambiguous: several purely geological or photochemical processes, researchers have shown, can under certain conditions generate similar atmospheric signatures without any biological activity whatsoever, meaning a detected signature alone cannot be treated as decisive proof.", "The James Webb Space Telescope, launched in 2021, has meaningfully advanced this search by analyzing the light that filters through a transiting planet's atmosphere during a transit, revealing which gases that atmosphere contains. Its instruments have already characterized the atmospheres of several large, close-orbiting exoplanets in considerable detail. Detecting comparable signals from smaller, Earth-sized planets, however, remains substantially more difficult, since such planets block far less starlight and produce correspondingly fainter atmospheric signals — a limitation that several astronomers argue will not be fully overcome until a new generation of even more sensitive instruments becomes operational.", "The TRAPPIST-1 system, discovered in 2017, illustrates both the promise and the complications this search regularly encounters. Seven roughly Earth-sized planets orbit a single red dwarf star, with as many as three positioned within the habitable zone — an unusually favorable arrangement for a single planetary system. Red dwarf stars, however, are also prone to powerful stellar flares considerably more frequent than those our own sun produces, and several researchers have argued that such flares may have stripped away the atmospheres of TRAPPIST-1's inner planets long ago, potentially undermining the very habitability their orbital position would otherwise suggest.", "Statistical extrapolation from these findings paints a striking picture at the galactic scale. Data gathered by the Kepler space telescope suggest that potentially rocky, habitable-zone planets could number in the billions across our galaxy alone, a figure that has reshaped how researchers frame the search for life. Despite this apparent statistical abundance, however, no research team has yet reported a confirmed biosignature detection, a gap between statistical promise and observational confirmation that most researchers in the field describe candidly as one of its central, unresolved tensions."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Exoplanet research has conclusively proved that habitability is widespread across nearby galaxies", "Exoplanet detection has advanced greatly, yet confirming actual habitability remains genuinely uncertain", "Red dwarf stars have been shown to be universally hostile to any form of planetary life", "Technological limitations have made traditional transit methods completely obsolete for current astronomy"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why does the transit method tend to favor planets that orbit close to their star?", options: ["Closer orbits reduce the total brightness dip that a transit produces", "Closer orbits make a planet's gravitational wobble easier for instruments to detect", "Closer orbits produce more frequent transits, which are easier to confirm reliably", "Closer orbits allow astronomers to photograph a planet's surface directly"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, why can the presence of oxygen and methane together not be treated as decisive proof of life?", options: ["Modern observational instruments remain incapable of detecting chemical compound signatures in remote space", "Neither gas has ever been identified within any extra-solar planetary atmosphere to date", "Certain geological or photochemical processes can also generate a similar signature", "These specific chemical compounds inherently neutralize each other whenever they come into direct contact"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as true of the James Webb Space Telescope?", options: ["It has successfully provided detailed spectral analyses for multiple large, close-orbiting exoplanets", "It evaluates starlight filtering through planetary atmospheres during transiting orbital events", "It can easily detect atmospheric signals from small, Earth-sized planets", "It was officially deployed and launched into service by international space agencies in 2021"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"replenishing\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["resembling", "revealing", "restocking", "removing"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"extrapolation\" in paragraph 7 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["projection", "exaggeration", "exploration", "confirmation"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The phrase \"such flares\" in paragraph 6 refers to _______.", options: ["the specific orbital placements of planets located directly inside theoretical habitable zones", "the collection of Earth-sized worlds discovered orbiting around the TRAPPIST-1 system", "the atmospheric gas compositions measured during planetary transit analyses", "the powerful stellar flares more frequent than the sun's own flares"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What complication do red dwarf stars pose for evaluating a planet's habitability?", options: ["Their extreme gravitational wobble prevents astronomers from measuring orbital parameters accurately", "Their surrounding habitable regions are theoretically incapable of supporting solid terrestrial bodies", "Their habitable-zone planets may face tidal locking and atmosphere-stripping stellar flares", "Their distance from Earth prevents ground-based observatories from recording any clear optical data"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred from paragraph 7 about the current state of the search for life on other planets?", options: ["The Kepler telescope has directly detected life on several rocky planets", "Statistical potential remains far ahead of any confirmed observational evidence so far", "Most researchers now consider the search for biosignatures essentially complete", "Researchers have already confirmed multiple biosignatures across several galaxy regions"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What is the author's overall attitude toward claims of confirmed life-bearing exoplanets?", options: ["Uninvolved and neutral, avoiding any direct assessment of recent observational achievements", "Entirely optimistic, accepting initial biosignature signals as definitive proof of alien life", "Highly skeptical, viewing exoplanet habitability research as largely ungrounded scientific speculation", "Cautious, emphasizing genuine progress alongside significant remaining scientific uncertainty"], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "Diverging Climate Trajectories Across Europe", paragraphs: ["Europe, taken as a whole, has warmed measurably faster than the global average over recent decades, a finding well supported by observational records. What this continental-scale figure obscures, however, is that the underlying regional experience of that warming is anything but uniform: a farmer in southern Spain and a fisherman in northern Norway are, in meaningful respects, contending with almost entirely different climatic trajectories, unified mainly by the shared statistical trend that made headlines.", "Southern and Mediterranean Europe illustrate one end of this divergence particularly starkly. Prolonged drought conditions, once considered periodic and recoverable, have in several Mediterranean regions become sufficiently persistent that agricultural planners now describe them as approaching a structural rather than a cyclical feature of the regional climate. Rising summer temperatures have compounded this pressure, extending wildfire seasons considerably beyond their historical bounds and placing increasing strain on water resources already stretched thin by agricultural and tourism demand alike.", "Northern and western Europe present an almost inverted picture. Rather than drying, several of these regions have experienced measurably increased winter precipitation, a pattern several climate scientists tentatively link to shifts in the jet stream's typical path, though the precise mechanism connecting a warming Arctic to these specific shifts remains, by the field's own admission, incompletely understood. The practical consequence has nonetheless been concrete: several northern European countries have recorded a marked rise in river flooding events over the past two decades, straining infrastructure originally designed for a considerably drier baseline.", "Mountain regions, meanwhile, face a distinct set of pressures tied less to precipitation patterns than to temperature alone. Alpine glaciers have retreated at a pace that has accelerated markedly since the 1980s, a trend with consequences reaching well beyond the mountains themselves, since meltwater from these glaciers historically sustained river flow through the drier summer months for tens of millions of people living downstream. Exactly how much further this retreat will progress depends heavily on which emissions trajectory the world ultimately follows, a dependency that leaves glaciologists able to offer only a plausible range of outcomes rather than a single confident projection; some downstream communities have already begun adapting their water storage infrastructure in anticipation, while others have been slower to act, partly because this dependency on unresolved emissions scenarios makes committing scarce public funds to any one projection politically difficult.", "Scandinavia and the broader Arctic periphery illustrate yet another distinct pattern, one frequently described as Arctic amplification: warming there has proceeded at a pace several times faster than the global average, driven by feedback loops involving melting sea ice and thawing permafrost. This accelerated warming carries consequences extending well beyond temperature readings alone, since infrastructure built on now-thawing permafrost has in several documented cases suffered structural damage, and traditional livelihoods dependent on stable seasonal ice patterns have grown correspondingly more precarious .", "Policy responses across the continent have, perhaps unsurprisingly, proven similarly uneven. While European Union frameworks establish continent-wide emissions targets, the practical work of adaptation — reinforcing flood defenses, redesigning agricultural practices, retrofitting infrastructure for a warmer climate — remains substantially a national and often municipal responsibility, and funding capacity for this work varies considerably between wealthier northern economies and more fiscally constrained regions further south and east, a disparity that several policy analysts argue risks entrenching precisely the climate vulnerabilities that adaptation funding is meant to address.", "Complicating any confident regional forecast further, climate models that perform relatively well at the global scale diverge considerably more sharply once applied to smaller geographic regions, since local topography, ocean currents, and atmospheric circulation patterns introduce sources of uncertainty that tend to average out at broader scales but remain stubbornly present at finer resolution. Attributing any single extreme weather event to climate change with full confidence therefore remains, climate scientists generally caution, a considerably more fraught exercise than confirming the broader warming trend itself.", "Taken together, this regional unevenness suggests that treating European climate change as a single, uniform phenomenon requiring a single continental strategy risks obscuring more than it reveals. The overarching warming trend is, by now, essentially beyond serious scientific dispute; how that warming will manifest in any particular region, by contrast, remains considerably less settled — a distinction that, several researchers argue, ought to shape adaptation policy considerably more than it currently does."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Southern European regions represent the primary geographical zone impacted by climate disruption", "European Union frameworks have effectively resolved structural climate adaptation challenges nationwide", "Every single European territory experiences identical manifestations of accelerated environmental warming", "Europe's overall warming trend is clear, but its regional effects vary considerably and unevenly"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, how have Mediterranean drought conditions changed in character over time?", options: ["They have shifted from periodic events toward a more persistent, structural pattern", "They now occur exclusively during the winter rather than summer months", "They have had no measurable effect on regional agricultural planning at all", "They have become considerably less frequent than they were several decades ago"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what remains incompletely understood about increased winter precipitation in northern and western Europe?", options: ["Whether river flooding events have actually increased over the past decades", "The precise mechanism linking Arctic warming to shifts in the jet stream", "Whether this precipitation pattern differs at all from Mediterranean Europe", "Whether infrastructure in these countries was designed for a wetter baseline"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a reason policy responses vary across Europe?", options: ["This disparity risks entrenching the vulnerabilities funding should address", "European Union frameworks currently set no continent-wide targets at all", "Funding capacity differs between wealthier and more constrained regions", "Adaptation work remains largely a national and municipal responsibility"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"precarious\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["predictable", "profitable", "unstable", "permanent"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"fraught\" in paragraph 7 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["difficult", "forgotten", "flawless", "frequent"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The phrase \"this dependency\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["how flooding events depend on aging northern European infrastructure", "how much further glacier retreat depends on future emissions trajectories", "how mountain regions depend on winter precipitation patterns", "how downstream populations depend on rivers for drinking water"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What does the passage suggest about confidently explaining specific regional climate patterns?", options: ["Regional mechanisms and specific events remain harder to confirm than the broader warming trend", "Localized meteorological extreme events can currently be linked directly to planetary climate shifts", "Predictive climate models demonstrate equivalent reliability across localized and continental spatial scales", "Localized environmental circulation patterns are understood with identical precision as broad global trends"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What can be inferred from paragraph 6 about the likely effect of uneven adaptation funding across Europe?", options: ["It has already been shown to eliminate all regional climate vulnerabilities", "It may deepen existing climate vulnerabilities in less fiscally resourced regions", "It affects wealthier northern economies more severely than southern regions", "It has no meaningful relationship to a region's climate vulnerability at all"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What is the author's overall purpose in writing this passage?", options: ["To challenge the scientific consensus regarding accelerated continental temperature increase rates", "To advocate for centralized European policy frameworks applying identical environmental standards", "To show that European climate change demands regionally tailored rather than uniform responses", "To demonstrate that Mediterranean territories constitute the only region facing severe ecological risk"], answer: 2 }] },
  ] },
];
